#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101760} :activity/id #uuid "cf46c580-819b-5052-9426-d777358df814" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048365 :topic/id #uuid "996292dd-e57e-5c8c-bce3-d584a7cf6144" :topic/math-academy-id 1164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101992 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the first-order differential equation\n\n$$\n2y\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}+y^2=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that it is *not* a first-order linear equation because it cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x) y = Q(x).$ In fact, this equation is an example of a *nonlinear* first-order differential equation.\n\nWe can sometimes transform a nonlinear differential equation into a linear differential equation using a substitution. If we can do this, then we can apply the method of integrating factors to solve it.\n\nTo transform the above equation into a linear differential equation, we can use the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}z} {\\textrm{d}x}=2y\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}z} {\\textrm{d}x}+z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear! So, we can use the method of integrating factors. The given equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=x.$ We then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=e^x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}{e}^{x}+z{e}^{x} & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =x{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate with respect to $x,$ using integration by parts to integrate the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x{e}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =(x-1){e}^{x}+C \\\\ z & =x-1+C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=y^2.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x-1+C{e}^{-x} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{x-1+C{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60bfb242-6329-5820-adf6-d96d5c970d57" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4060 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "caa22dea-6722-5c27-aa99-c035d6beef32" :step/math-academy-id 24251 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100759 :question/id #uuid "4d942842-ec76-5ea5-9591-e8b8b8732425" :question/math-academy-id "e-6605"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436376}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "d4ce90c3-1349-518a-a9d8-ac07ccd89f84" :step/math-academy-id 24252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100760 :question/id #uuid "53e6ba68-a2bc-5761-b32f-4b01057ea925" :question/math-academy-id "e-6606"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569}] :knowl